## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical understanding of timer-driven thread wakeups, race condition handling, and efficient data structures in a kernel-like environment. It tests a candidate's ability to design a concrete mechanism that works correctly under contention while maintaining precise timing guarantees.

## Key Concepts

- Sleep queues and timer interrupt handling
- Min-heap data structure for efficient wake-time ordering
- Concurrency safety and time rechecking to mitigate timer jitter
- Correctness guarantees: ensuring no missed wakeups and exact timing

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal sketch of a per-CPU sleep queue
typedef struct SleepEntry {
  uint64_t wake_at;
  int tid;
  struct SleepEntry *next;
} SleepEntry;

// Enqueue thread for timed sleep
void sleep(int tid, uint64_t duration) {
  uint64_t now = get_time();
  SleepEntry entry = {now + duration, tid};
  minheap_insert(&cpu->sleep_queue, entry);
  block_thread(tid);
}

// Timer interrupt handler
void timer_interrupt() {
  uint64_t now = get_time();
  while (!minheap_empty(&cpu->sleep_queue)) {
    SleepEntry entry = minheap_peek(&cpu->sleep_queue);
    if (entry.wake_at > now) break;

    entry = minheap_pop(&cpu->sleep_queue);
    if (entry.wake_at <= now) {
      wake_thread(entry.tid);
    } else {
      minheap_insert(&cpu->sleep_queue, entry);
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to reason about scalable scheduler design under high concurrency and NUMA-aware memory behavior, reflecting real-world cloud and fintech workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Per-CPU run queues enabling lock-free enqueue/dequeue operations
- Dynamic priority boosting based on wait-time and latency telemetry
- NUMA-aware task placement to minimize memory access latency
- Global I/O-priority bitmap for efficient priority scanning
- Lock-free enqueue using compare-and-swap (CAS) primitives

## Validation Approach
Fairness and throughput under contention can be validated through:
1. **Fairness testing**: Measure wait time distribution across task types using synthetic workloads with controlled I/O and CPU-bound ratios
2. **Throughput benchmarking**: Run high-concurrency workloads and measure tasks completed per second
3. **NUMA locality analysis**: Track remote vs. local memory accesses using hardware counters
4. **Starvation detection**: Verify I/O-bound tasks receive CPU time within bounded latency under sustained CPU contention
5. **Load balance profiling**: Monitor task migration frequency and queue depth across CPUs

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Task {
    atomic_int priority;
    u64 wait_time;
    u64 last_io_latency;
    struct Task *next;
} Task;

typedef struct PerCpuQueue {
    Task *head;
    atomic_T tail;
} PerCpuQueue;

void enqueue(Task *t) {
    PerCpuQueue *q = &per_cpu_queues[smp_processor_id()];
    Task *old_tail = atomic_load(&q->tail);
    do {
        t->next = old_tail;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange(&q->tail, &old_tail, t));
}

Task *dequeue(void) {
    int cpu = find_highest_priority_cpu();
    PerCpuQueue *q = &per_cpu_queues[cpu];
    return atomic_fetch_head(&q->head);
}

void rebalance(void) {
    for_each_cpu(cpu) {
        if (queue_depth(cpu) > threshold)
            migrate_to_neighbor(cpu);
    }
}
```

